WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.734
Renata's coding agent handles long tasks.

00:00:05.034 --> 00:00:06.608
Two or three hours.

00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:11.254
Migrating a module, upgrading dependencies, fixing a batch of tests.

00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:14.542
At the start of a task, it's precise.

00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:20.099
After one hundred fifty turns, the context holds hundreds of tool results.

00:00:20.399 --> 00:00:25.653
Whole files, test output thousands of lines long, searches that led nowhere.

00:00:26.303 --> 00:00:30.418
Near the end, the agent starts editing files it already fixed.

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:35.460
Reapplying changes that someone had already reverted, on purpose.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.238
And forgetting the constraint agreed at the start.

00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:41.412
Don't touch the public API.

00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:45.468
On some tasks, the window overflows, and the run fails.

00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:48.456
Starting a fresh session doesn't help.

00:00:48.756 --> 00:00:53.108
The agent would lose the plan, the decisions, and what's been done.

00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:58.547
In this lesson, you'll learn to keep an agent clear headed on a task longer than its

00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:00.015
context window can hold.

00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:04.949
Treat the context as working memory, with ongoing maintenance.

00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:07.781
Three operations, in order of cost.

00:01:08.431 --> 00:01:09.015
Prune.

00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:14.219
Delete the contents of old tool results that were already used, like a file read

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:15.261
eighty turns ago.

00:01:15.561 --> 00:01:16.926
The call stays on record.

00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:18.612
The content goes.

00:01:19.262 --> 00:01:20.158
Summarize.

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:25.802
When the context passes a limit, replace the old part with a summary that keeps the

00:01:25.802 --> 00:01:29.601
goal, the decisions, the current state, and the next steps.

00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:31.472
And externalize.

00:01:31.772 --> 00:01:37.232
Facts that must survive any summary, like the plan, the constraints, and the list of

00:01:37.232 --> 00:01:40.937
changed files, go to a file or state outside the context.

00:01:41.237 --> 00:01:43.687
The agent reads them when it needs to.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:47.761
The trigger is a token limit, checked before each turn.

00:01:48.061 --> 00:01:50.686
The most recent turns always stay whole.

00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:52.941
And here's the division of labor.

00:01:53.241 --> 00:01:55.261
Code decides when to cut.

00:01:55.561 --> 00:01:57.799
The model only writes the summary.

00:01:58.999 --> 00:02:00.705
The agent works as usual.

00:02:01.355 --> 00:02:04.550
Before each turn, code measures the context.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.025
The first measure is cheap.

00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:10.190
Delete the contents of old tool results.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.674
If pruning was enough, the loop continues.

00:02:14.324 --> 00:02:18.776
If not, a model summarizes everything before the latest turns.

00:02:19.426 --> 00:02:24.969
And critical facts live outside the context, in a notes file or in state.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:26.863
The summary points to them.

00:02:28.063 --> 00:02:29.233
Back to Renata.

00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:34.969
On turn three, she agreed: don't change the public API of the billing module.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:40.350
On turn eighty, the agent read the billing API file, about two thousand four hundred

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.826
tokens, and ran the tests, about one thousand eight hundred tokens of log.

00:02:45.126 --> 00:02:50.540
On turn one hundred fifty, those results still ride along on every call, with dozens

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.185
of others.

00:02:51.835 --> 00:02:57.053
With illustrative numbers, by turn one hundred fifty, input already passes sixty

00:02:57.053 --> 00:02:58.684
thousand tokens per call.

00:02:58.984 --> 00:03:03.067
Without pruning, the next fifty turns resend that volume.

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:07.549
About three million input tokens, just to finish the task.

00:03:08.199 --> 00:03:10.947
Pruning swaps the old content for one line.

00:03:11.247 --> 00:03:13.757
The history still says the read happened.

00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:16.774
The two thousand four hundred tokens leave.

00:03:17.074 --> 00:03:20.503
And if the agent needs the file again, it reads it again.

00:03:20.803 --> 00:03:23.790
That's cheaper than carrying the file for fifty turns.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.408
When pruning isn't enough, the summary comes in.

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:30.251
Here's a schema that holds up in Renata's eval.

00:03:30.551 --> 00:03:31.211
The goal.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:32.422
Decisions.

00:03:32.722 --> 00:03:33.563
What's done.

00:03:33.863 --> 00:03:35.030
The current state.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.364
The next steps.

00:03:37.014 --> 00:03:38.041
And this line.

00:03:38.341 --> 00:03:39.221
Constraints.

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:42.093
Do not change the public API.

00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:44.417
Here's one for you.

00:03:44.717 --> 00:03:49.057
After a compaction, Renata's agent changes a public signature.

00:03:49.357 --> 00:03:53.170
The summary had the goal, what's done, and the next steps.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.930
But no constraints field.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.193
What's the fix?

00:03:59.243 --> 00:04:04.131
Put constraints in the summary schema, and in a notes file outside the context.

00:04:04.431 --> 00:04:09.534
Anything that must survive every cut needs a place in the summary, and a copy

00:04:09.534 --> 00:04:10.263
outside it.

00:04:10.563 --> 00:04:14.033
A goal and next steps don't store a do not.

00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:19.737
Raising the limit so compaction never runs only delays the rot.

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:24.035
And a bigger model doesn't remember what you removed from the context.

00:04:24.335 --> 00:04:29.319
Without that field, the compactor helps you forget the one thing that must not be

00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:29.934
forgotten.

00:04:31.134 --> 00:04:32.230
Short tasks.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.647
The compaction step costs more than it saves.

00:04:36.297 --> 00:04:38.913
Heavy reading in a well bounded subtask.

00:04:39.213 --> 00:04:40.826
Isolate it in a subagent.

00:04:41.126 --> 00:04:43.679
Sometimes the two patterns go together.

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:46.730
Isolation keeps heavy material out.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.444
Compaction cleans what already got in.

00:04:50.094 --> 00:04:56.023
Content that must be read in full for the answer, like a contract reviewed clause by

00:04:56.023 --> 00:04:56.517
clause.

00:04:56.817 --> 00:04:59.749
Summarizing there loses what matters.

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:01.825
And a short support chat.

00:05:02.125 --> 00:05:07.100
Say it ends in eight turns, and a colleague wants to compact every four, to save

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:07.473
money.

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.458
Don't.

00:05:09.758 --> 00:05:15.048
The history fits, and a summary only risks losing the original request, like the

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:17.296
order number in the first message.

00:05:17.596 --> 00:05:20.823
Keep those facts as explicit state instead.

00:05:21.473 --> 00:05:22.958
Now the common traps.

00:05:23.258 --> 00:05:25.113
Summarizing too early.

00:05:25.413 --> 00:05:30.966
Compacting every few turns spends a call, and deletes detail that would still be

00:05:30.966 --> 00:05:31.313
used.

00:05:31.613 --> 00:05:32.391
Measure first.

00:05:32.691 --> 00:05:34.832
Cut when the token curve hurts.

00:05:35.482 --> 00:05:37.392
A schema with no constraints.

00:05:37.692 --> 00:05:39.163
We just saw that one.

00:05:39.463 --> 00:05:42.052
Your retention eval needs a case like it.

00:05:42.702 --> 00:05:45.130
Cutting in the middle of a tool call.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:50.771
Separate a tool request from its result, and the next API request is invalid.

00:05:51.071 --> 00:05:53.273
The cut starts on a user turn.

00:05:53.923 --> 00:05:56.054
And throwing the raw history away.

00:05:56.354 --> 00:05:59.884
You'll need it to debug a summary that invented a decision.

00:06:00.184 --> 00:06:04.160
Compaction changes what the model sees, not what you keep.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.545
One question teams always ask.

00:06:07.845 --> 00:06:13.776
After pruning, the agent rereads a file it already read eighty turns ago.

00:06:14.076 --> 00:06:15.021
Is that a bug?

00:06:15.321 --> 00:06:15.989
No.

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:18.491
Pruned content is gone on purpose.

00:06:18.791 --> 00:06:21.971
If the file still matters, the tool is called again.

00:06:22.271 --> 00:06:25.235
And the summary shouldn't copy whole files either.

00:06:25.535 --> 00:06:28.744
It stores decisions and state, not source code.

00:06:29.394 --> 00:06:30.988
How do you evaluate it?

00:06:31.288 --> 00:06:32.536
Retention first.

00:06:32.836 --> 00:06:37.800
Ask questions about facts from early in the task, after compaction.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.138
That's the eval most people skip.

00:06:40.438 --> 00:06:44.802
Without it, compaction works on cost, and fails on the product.

00:06:45.452 --> 00:06:48.081
Then the completion rate, and rework.

00:06:48.381 --> 00:06:51.157
Files edited twice, for the same reason.

00:06:51.807 --> 00:06:54.437
Then tokens per turn, over the run.

00:06:54.737 --> 00:06:57.669
The curve should have teeth, not climb forever.

00:06:57.969 --> 00:07:03.519
And if the tokens before and after a compaction barely change, the old messages were

00:07:03.519 --> 00:07:04.972
never really replaced.

00:07:05.622 --> 00:07:06.799
And the prompt cache.

00:07:07.099 --> 00:07:10.027
Changing the start of the context can reset it.

00:07:10.327 --> 00:07:13.125
Watch the cached tokens after each compaction.

00:07:13.775 --> 00:07:16.622
Some providers already do part of this for you.

00:07:16.922 --> 00:07:21.770
Server side compaction, where the API summarizes old context on its own.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:27.407
Or context editing, where the API clears old tool results before the model sees

00:07:27.407 --> 00:07:27.745
them.

00:07:28.045 --> 00:07:30.323
Prefer those when they cover your case.

00:07:30.623 --> 00:07:32.202
And one privacy note.

00:07:32.502 --> 00:07:35.925
The summary and the notes file often end up in logs.

00:07:36.225 --> 00:07:41.583
If the conversation holds personal data, compaction creates one more copy.

00:07:41.883 --> 00:07:43.935
Restrict who can read those logs.

00:07:45.135 --> 00:07:47.462
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:47.762 --> 00:07:51.785
A long task needs a maintained memory, not a growing pile.

00:07:52.085 --> 00:07:58.411
Prune what's been used, summarize the rest into a schema that has room for every do

00:07:58.411 --> 00:08:03.365
not, and keep the critical facts in a file no summary can delete.

00:08:03.665 --> 00:08:06.006
Then hour three looks like minute three.

00:08:06.656 --> 00:08:12.162
In the next lesson, a smell about the shape of the agent itself: one agent trying to

00:08:12.162 --> 00:08:13.080
do everything.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.512
The mega agent.

00:08:14.812 --> 00:08:15.575
See you there.
